# keur quality control — a JS/TS repo whose sonar-project.properties configures
# no LCOV coverage report path (Sonar then shows 0% coverage).
#
# Why this exists: for JavaScript/TypeScript, Sonar reads coverage from an LCOV
# report via `sonar.javascript.lcov.reportPaths` (the same key covers TS). Miss
# it and coverage reads 0% and any coverage gate fails silently.
#
# HOW (requires + absence): `requires: lang: js ts` gates to repos containing JS
# or TS (facts, ADR-009). The absence engine fires when the lcov key appears
# NOWHERE. `content_contains: sonar.sources` skips files that configure no
# analysis. The per-language refinement: keur knows it is JS/TS, so it demands
# the lcov key specifically.
#
# WARN, low confidence: coverage may be passed via CI or deliberately skipped.
# @see docs/rules-authoring.md
id: SONAR-006
ilities: [testability]
maturity: 3
title: JS/TS repo — Sonar config has no LCOV coverage report path
category: quality
severity: warning
engine: absence
action: warn
confidence: 0.5
requires:
  lang: js ts
target:
  filenames: sonar-project.properties
  content_contains: sonar.sources
patterns:
  - regex: (?mi)^\s*sonar\.(javascript|typescript)\.lcov\.reportPaths\s*=\s*\S
    message: "JS/TS repo: sonar-project.properties sets sonar.sources but no sonar.javascript.lcov.reportPaths — Sonar will show 0% coverage. Point it at your lcov.info."
fix: "Add `sonar.javascript.lcov.reportPaths=coverage/lcov.info` (the key also covers TypeScript), and make sure your test runner emits lcov (e.g. jest --coverage, vitest --coverage) before the scan."
